{"id": 77, "ts": 1780432886.4616961, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 5, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fB is the strongest overall fit, given the clear evidence of high reliability (very conscientious), excellent stress resilience (handles stress very well), and solid technical competence (good at assessing weather conditions and excellent computer skills). Those attributes directly address the core safety\u2011critical criteria for long\u2011distance operations.\nHowever, we still need to resolve the lingering concerns about crew cooperation and professional communication \u2013 the pretentious perception and occasional wrong tone could affect cockpit harmony. At the same time, Candidate\u202fD brings impressive problem\u2011solving, responsibility, concentration, and an ability to respond adequately to unexpected events, but the notes that D is \u201cconsidered moody\u201d and \u201cregarded as a loner\u201d raise similar crew\u2011cooperation red flags.\nCould anyone share additional observations on how Candidate\u202fD interacts with crew members in simulated or real\u2011world settings? Specifically, do we have any evidence that mitigates the \u201cloner\u201d and \u201cmoody\u201d concerns, or that demonstrates effective teamwork under pressure?"}
